<% govspeak_for :body do %>
  This calculator is for students living in England who are starting:

  - a new undergraduate funded course in academic year 2025 to 2026, or 2026 to 2027

  - a course or module from levels 4 to 6 (and some courses from level 7) on or after 1 January 2027

  There’s a different way to check how much you can get if you’re [a student from Scotland](https://www.saas.gov.uk/), [a student from Wales](https://www.studentfinancewales.co.uk/) or [a student from Northern Ireland](https://www.studentfinanceni.co.uk/).

  Use this calculator to estimate the amount you could get each year in:

  - Tuition Fee Loans to help pay your course tuition fees
  - Maintenance Loans to help with your living costs
  - extra funding, for example if you’re on a low income, are disabled, have children or are studying a certain course
  
  ## Before you start

  You may need to know your annual household income (your parents’ or partner’s income). There’s different guidance on household income depending on whether:

  - [your course starts before 1 January 2027](/apply-for-student-finance/household-income)
  - [your course starts on or after 1 January 2027](/apply-for-student-finance-lifelong-learning-entitlement/household-income)

  You need to know if your course is full-time or part-time, and how many credits it's worth. If you're studying part-time, you need to know how many credits your course is worth full-time as well as how many credits you'll be studying.

  A year of studying a bachelor’s (undergraduate) degree full time is usually worth 120 credits.

  You cannot use this calculator if you are studying a module worth less than 30 credits.

  ##Calculate how much you could get
<% end %>
